# BUG-287 regression: inside a method, a bare `name()` is a SIBLING METHOD before it is
# a same-named global type.
#
# Before the fix, `genCall` tested its class-constructor branch before its owner-method
# branch, so `Widget()` inside `User.callsIt` emitted `_zbr_ty_Widget.init()` — it
# CONSTRUCTED the class instead of calling the method. The program then failed to
# compile with `unused function parameter`, because `self` was never read once the
# sibling call had been rewritten away. A message pointing nowhere near the cause.
#
# BOTH LEGS MATTER, and the second is the one that makes this a real test. A blind
# reordering of the two branches would pass leg 1 and break every ordinary constructor
# call in the corpus; leg 2 is what catches that. The fix is a guard rather than a
# reorder, so it can only fire when a type and a sibling method share one name.
#
# Found 2026-08-13 writing the `error`/`try` fixture, by a shape no corpus program had:
# a class and a method carrying the same name. Reduced to ordinary names to prove it was
# not caused by freeing a keyword — it was not.
#
# NOTE this is NOT the same as a field and a method sharing a name. That collides inside
# a single emitted struct and is caught cleanly at the Zebra level as
# `duplicate struct member name`.

class Widget
    var n: int = 42

    def get(): int
        return .n

class User
    var base: int = 3

    # leg 1: a method whose name is also a class in this file
    def Widget(): int
        return .base + 10

    def callsIt(): int
        return Widget() + 100

class Plain
    # leg 2: no same-named method here, so the bare call must still CONSTRUCT
    def build(): int
        return Widget().get()

def main()
    print(User().callsIt())      # 113 — the sibling method won
    print(Plain().build())       # 42  — the constructor still constructs
    print("bug287: OK")
